window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2024-12-3", "source": "2024年12月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose the university newspaper is inviting submissions from the students for its coming edition on how the students can contribute to a green campus. You are now to write an essay for submission. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "A green campus is no longer a slogan but a shared responsibility, and students play a key role in making it real.\nAs members of the campus, we can turn small daily habits into real changes.\nThere are several practical things we can do. First, we should sort our rubbish and recycle paper, bottles and\ncans, which cuts waste and saves resources. Second, saving water and switching off lights and computers when\nthey are not in use can greatly reduce the energy our campus consumes. Moreover, choosing to walk, cycle or\nshare bikes for short trips lowers carbon emissions, and passing used textbooks on to younger students gives\nthem a second life instead of being thrown away.\nIn short, a green campus grows out of these everyday actions rather than empty talk. If every student takes one\nsmall step, our campus will surely become cleaner, greener and more livable for us all."}, "translation": {"prompt": "敦煌莫高窟 (Mogao Grottoes) 数字展示中心于2014年开放启用， 是莫高窟保护利用工程的重要组成部分。展示中心采用数字技术和多媒体展示手段， 使游客进入洞窟参观之前就能了解莫高窟的历史文化， 鉴赏莫高窟的艺术经典。这将减少开放洞窟的数量， 缩短游客在洞窟内的逗留时间， 减轻参观对莫高窟造成的影响， 以使这一世界文化遗产得到妥善保护、长久利用。", "sample": "The Mogao Grottoes Digital Exhibition Center, which opened in 2014, is an important part of the project for the protection and utilization of the Mogao Grottoes. The center adopts digital technology and multimedia means, enabling visitors to learn about the history and culture of the Mogao Grottoes and appreciate its artistic classics before they enter the caves. This will reduce the number of open caves, shorten the time visitors stay inside, and lessen the impact of visits on the Mogao Grottoes, so that this World Cultural Heritage site can be properly protected and used over the long term. 第1句已给出全称 The Mogao Grottoes Digital Exhibition Center，第2句再说展示中心时不必重"}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "Super realistic masks are made from flexible materials such as silicone and are designed to imitate real human face—down to every last detail.\nIn a study by the Universities of York and Kyoto, researchers asked participants to look at pairs of photographs and decide which showed a  __26__  face and which showed a person wearing a mask.\nSurprisingly, participants made the wrong call in one-in-five cases.\nThe 20% error rate  __27__  in the study likely underestimates the extent to which people would struggle to tell an  __28__  face from the real thing outside of the lab.\nThe researchers collected data from participants from both the UK and Japan to  __29__  any differences according to race. When trial participants were asked to choose between photographs of faces of a different race from theirs, response times were slower and selections were 5% less  __30__  .\nThere are now dozens of criminal cases in which offenders have  __31__  themselves off as people of a different age, race or gender, sending police investigations down the wrong path.\nIn one recent case, an international gang used an  __32__  realistic mask to pose as a French minister,  __33__  business executives out of millions of pounds.\nDr Jet Sanders, who worked on the study while a PhD student at the University of York, said: \"Failure to detect synthetic faces may have important implications for security and crime prevention as super realistic masks may  __34__  the key characteristics of a person's appearance to be  __35__  identified.\"", "matchIntro": "Stop thinking and start doing", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Most people have a dream in life, a vision of who or what they'd like to be in the future. At a minimum, everyone has interests and values that determine what they want out of their lives. Even so, trying to set achievable goals that you'll work for over the course of many years can be extremely challenging. It can be hard to know where to even begin, and the things you hope to achieve may seem impossible. But, if you're well prepared you may be able to set goals for your life that are just as fulfilling to work toward as to achieve."}, {"label": "B", "text": "We all have goals that we want to achieve in our lives. These goals may include learning a new language, eating healthier and losing weight, becoming a better parent, saving more money, and so on. It can be easy to assume that the gap between where you are now and where you want to be in the future is caused by a lack of knowledge. This is why we buy courses on how to start a business or how to lose weight fast or how to learn a new language in three months. We assume that if we knew about a better strategy, then we would get better results. We believe that a new result requires new knowledge."}, {"label": "C", "text": "What I'm starting to realize, however, is that new knowledge does not necessarily drive new results. In fact, learning something new can actually be a waste of time if your goal is to make progress and not simply gain additional knowledge."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It all comes down to the difference between learning and practicing. Thomas Sterner, in his book *The Practicing Mind*, explains the key difference between practicing and learning. He says: \"When we practice something, we are involved in the deliberate repetition of a process with the intention of reaching a specific goal.\" Here the words \"deliberate\" and \"intention\" are key, because they define the difference between actively practicing something and passively learning it."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Learning something new and practicing something new may seem very similar, but these two methods can have profoundly different results. Here are some additional ways to think about the difference. Let's say your goal is to get stronger and more fit. You can research the best instructions on weight lifting technique, but the only way to build strength is to practice lifting weights. Let's say your goal is to grow your startup. You can learn about the best way to make a sales pitch, but the only way to actually land customers is to practice making sales calls. Let's say your goal is to write a book. You can talk to a best-selling author about writing, but the only way to become a better writer is to practice publishing consistently."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Passive learning creates knowledge. Active practice creates skill. Let's consider a couple more reasons to prioritize active practice over passive learning. First, passive learning can be a stick that supports inaction. In many cases, learning is actually a way to avoid taking action on the goals and interests that we say are important to us. For example, let's say you want to learn a foreign language. Reading a book on how to learn a foreign language quickly allows you to feel like you are making progress (\"Hey, I'm figuring out the best way to do this!\"). Of course, you're not actually practicing the action that would deliver your desired outcome (speaking the foreign language)."}, {"label": "G", "text": "In situations like this one, we often claim that we are preparing or researching the best method, but these rationalizations allow us to feel like we are moving forward when we are merely spinning our wheels. We make the mistake of being in motion rather than taking action. Learning is valuable until it becomes a form of procrastination(拖延)."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Another point to consider is that practice is learning, but learning is not practice. Passive learning is not a form of practice because although you gain new knowledge, you are not discovering how to apply that knowledge. Active practice, meanwhile, is one of the greatest forms of learning because the mistakes you make while practicing reveal important insights."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Even more importantly, practice is the only way to make a meaningful contribution with your knowledge. You can watch an online course about how to build a business or read an article about a terrible disaster in a developing nation, but that knowledge is unproductive unless you actually launch your business or donate to those in need. Learning by itself can be valuable for you, but if you want to be valuable to others, then you have to express your knowledge in some way."}, {"label": "J", "text": "The last important point to consider is that practice focuses your energy on the process. Thomas Sterner once said, \"Progress is a natural result of staying focused on the process of doing anything.\" The state of your life right now is a result of the habits and beliefs that you have been practicing each day. When you realize this and begin to direct your focus toward practicing better habits day-in and day-out, continual progress will be the logical outcome. It is not the things we learn nor the dreams we visualize that determine our results, but rather the habits that we practice each day. Fall in love with boredom and focus your energy on the process, not the product."}, {"label": "K", "text": "What does this all mean? Is passive learning useless? Of course not. In many cases, learning for the sake of learning can be a beautiful thing. Not to mention that soaking up new information can help you make more informed decisions when you do decide to take action. That said, the main point of this article is that learning by itself does not lead to progress. We often hide behind information and use learning as an excuse to delay the more difficult and more important choice of actually doing something. Spend less time passively learning and more time actively practicing. Stop thinking and start doing."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Research in human-vehicle interaction has shown even systems designed to automate driving are far from being error-proof. Recent evidence points to drivers' limited understanding of what these systems can and cannot do as a contributing factor to system misuse. A recent study tackles the issue of over-trusting drivers and the resulting system misuse from a legal viewpoint. It looks at what the manufacturers of self-driving cars should legally do to ensure that drivers understand how to use the vehicles appropriately.\nOne solution suggested in the study involves requiring buyers to sign end-user license agreements (EULAs), similar to the terms and conditions that require agreement when using new software products. But this is far from ideal. The agreement may not provide enough information to the driver, leading to confusion about the nature of the requests for agreement and their implications. Further, most end users don't read EULAs. A 2017 study shows 91 percent of people agree to them without reading. Among young people, 97 percent agree without reviewing the terms.\nThe issue is that, unlike using a smartphone app, operating a car has serious safety risks, whether the driver is human or software. And human drivers need to consent to take responsibility for the outcomes of the software and hardware.\n\"Warning fatigue\" and distracted driving are also causes for concern. For example, a driver, annoyed after receiving continuous warnings, could decide to just ignore the message. Or, if the message is presented while the vehicle is in motion, it could represent a distraction. Given these limitations and concerns, even if this mode of obtaining consent is to move forward, it likely won't fully protect automakers from their legal liability should the system malfunction(发生故障) or an accident occur.\nDriver training for self-driving vehicles can help ensure that drivers fully understand system capabilities and limitations. This needs to occur beyond the vehicle purchase. Recent evidence shows even relying on the information provided by the seller is not going to answer many questions. All of this considered, the road forward for self-driving cars is not going to be a smooth ride after all.", "47": "Research in human-vehicle interaction has shown even systems designed to automate driving are far from being error-proof. Recent evidence points to drivers' limited understanding of what these systems can and cannot do as a contributing factor to system misuse. A recent study tackles the issue of over-trusting drivers and the resulting system misuse from a legal viewpoint. It looks at what the manufacturers of self-driving cars should legally do to ensure that drivers understand how to use the vehicles appropriately.\nOne solution suggested in the study involves requiring buyers to sign end-user license agreements (EULAs), similar to the terms and conditions that require agreement when using new software products. But this is far from ideal. The agreement may not provide enough information to the driver, leading to confusion about the nature of the requests for agreement and their implications. Further, most end users don't read EULAs. A 2017 study shows 91 percent of people agree to them without reading. Among young people, 97 percent agree without reviewing the terms.\nThe issue is that, unlike using a smartphone app, operating a car has serious safety risks, whether the driver is human or software. And human drivers need to consent to take responsibility for the outcomes of the software and hardware.\n\"Warning fatigue\" and distracted driving are also causes for concern. For example, a driver, annoyed after receiving continuous warnings, could decide to just ignore the message. Or, if the message is presented while the vehicle is in motion, it could represent a distraction. Given these limitations and concerns, even if this mode of obtaining consent is to move forward, it likely won't fully protect automakers from their legal liability should the system malfunction(发生故障) or an accident occur.\nDriver training for self-driving vehicles can help ensure that drivers fully understand system capabilities and limitations. This needs to occur beyond the vehicle purchase. Recent evidence shows even relying on the information provided by the seller is not going to answer many questions. All of this considered, the road forward for self-driving cars is not going to be a smooth ride after all.", "48": "Research in human-vehicle interaction has shown even systems designed to automate driving are far from being error-proof. Recent evidence points to drivers' limited understanding of what these systems can and cannot do as a contributing factor to system misuse. A recent study tackles the issue of over-trusting drivers and the resulting system misuse from a legal viewpoint. It looks at what the manufacturers of self-driving cars should legally do to ensure that drivers understand how to use the vehicles appropriately.\nOne solution suggested in the study involves requiring buyers to sign end-user license agreements (EULAs), similar to the terms and conditions that require agreement when using new software products. But this is far from ideal. The agreement may not provide enough information to the driver, leading to confusion about the nature of the requests for agreement and their implications. Further, most end users don't read EULAs. A 2017 study shows 91 percent of people agree to them without reading. Among young people, 97 percent agree without reviewing the terms.\nThe issue is that, unlike using a smartphone app, operating a car has serious safety risks, whether the driver is human or software. And human drivers need to consent to take responsibility for the outcomes of the software and hardware.\n\"Warning fatigue\" and distracted driving are also causes for concern. For example, a driver, annoyed after receiving continuous warnings, could decide to just ignore the message. Or, if the message is presented while the vehicle is in motion, it could represent a distraction. Given these limitations and concerns, even if this mode of obtaining consent is to move forward, it likely won't fully protect automakers from their legal liability should the system malfunction(发生故障) or an accident occur.\nDriver training for self-driving vehicles can help ensure that drivers fully understand system capabilities and limitations. This needs to occur beyond the vehicle purchase. Recent evidence shows even relying on the information provided by the seller is not going to answer many questions. All of this considered, the road forward for self-driving cars is not going to be a smooth ride after all.", "49": "Research in human-vehicle interaction has shown even systems designed to automate driving are far from being error-proof. Recent evidence points to drivers' limited understanding of what these systems can and cannot do as a contributing factor to system misuse. A recent study tackles the issue of over-trusting drivers and the resulting system misuse from a legal viewpoint. It looks at what the manufacturers of self-driving cars should legally do to ensure that drivers understand how to use the vehicles appropriately.\nOne solution suggested in the study involves requiring buyers to sign end-user license agreements (EULAs), similar to the terms and conditions that require agreement when using new software products. But this is far from ideal. The agreement may not provide enough information to the driver, leading to confusion about the nature of the requests for agreement and their implications. Further, most end users don't read EULAs. A 2017 study shows 91 percent of people agree to them without reading. Among young people, 97 percent agree without reviewing the terms.\nThe issue is that, unlike using a smartphone app, operating a car has serious safety risks, whether the driver is human or software. And human drivers need to consent to take responsibility for the outcomes of the software and hardware.\n\"Warning fatigue\" and distracted driving are also causes for concern. For example, a driver, annoyed after receiving continuous warnings, could decide to just ignore the message. Or, if the message is presented while the vehicle is in motion, it could represent a distraction. Given these limitations and concerns, even if this mode of obtaining consent is to move forward, it likely won't fully protect automakers from their legal liability should the system malfunction(发生故障) or an accident occur.\nDriver training for self-driving vehicles can help ensure that drivers fully understand system capabilities and limitations. This needs to occur beyond the vehicle purchase. Recent evidence shows even relying on the information provided by the seller is not going to answer many questions. All of this considered, the road forward for self-driving cars is not going to be a smooth ride after all.", "50": "Research in human-vehicle interaction has shown even systems designed to automate driving are far from being error-proof. Recent evidence points to drivers' limited understanding of what these systems can and cannot do as a contributing factor to system misuse. A recent study tackles the issue of over-trusting drivers and the resulting system misuse from a legal viewpoint. It looks at what the manufacturers of self-driving cars should legally do to ensure that drivers understand how to use the vehicles appropriately.\nOne solution suggested in the study involves requiring buyers to sign end-user license agreements (EULAs), similar to the terms and conditions that require agreement when using new software products. But this is far from ideal. The agreement may not provide enough information to the driver, leading to confusion about the nature of the requests for agreement and their implications. Further, most end users don't read EULAs. A 2017 study shows 91 percent of people agree to them without reading. Among young people, 97 percent agree without reviewing the terms.\nThe issue is that, unlike using a smartphone app, operating a car has serious safety risks, whether the driver is human or software. And human drivers need to consent to take responsibility for the outcomes of the software and hardware.\n\"Warning fatigue\" and distracted driving are also causes for concern. For example, a driver, annoyed after receiving continuous warnings, could decide to just ignore the message. Or, if the message is presented while the vehicle is in motion, it could represent a distraction. Given these limitations and concerns, even if this mode of obtaining consent is to move forward, it likely won't fully protect automakers from their legal liability should the system malfunction(发生故障) or an accident occur.\nDriver training for self-driving vehicles can help ensure that drivers fully understand system capabilities and limitations. This needs to occur beyond the vehicle purchase. Recent evidence shows even relying on the information provided by the seller is not going to answer many questions. All of this considered, the road forward for self-driving cars is not going to be a smooth ride after all.", "51": "Do you ever blend up a protein drink for breakfast, or grab a protein bar following an afternoon workout? If so, you are likely among the millions of people in search of more protein-rich diets.\nProtein-enriched products are found everywhere. But contrary to all the publicity that everyone needs more protein, most Americans get twice as much as they need.\nMany of us living in the most developed countries are buying into a myth of protein deficiency created by food companies and self-identified health experts. Global retail sales of protein supplement products reached an astonishing US$18.9 billion in 2020.\nBut are we really in need of more protein? Physicians in the U.S. have never actually examined a patient with protein deficiency because simply by eating an adequate number of daily calories we are also most likely getting enough protein.\nIn fact, Americans currently consume almost twice the National Academy of Medicine's recommended daily intake of protein although the most desirable protein intake may vary depending on age and activity level.\nFor example, if you're a dedicated athlete you might need to consume higher quantities of protein. Generally, though, a 140-pound person should not exceed 120 grams of protein per day, particularly because a high protein diet can strain kidney and liver function and increase risks of developing heart disease and cancer.\nWhile fats and sugar have taken the beating in turns since over a century ago, protein has managed to remain our red-hot favorite.\nIn the 1970s through the 1990s, protein products remained visible but moved back somewhat with the dietary spotlight firmly fixed on low-calorie, low-fat, sugar-free snack foods and beverages following the publication of studies linking sugar and saturated(饱和的) fat consumption to heart disease.\nLater research in 2003, however, suggested high-protein diets could aid in weight loss, and protein quickly regained its former nutrient-superstar status.\nNow most people living in high-income nations are consuming enough protein. When we replace meals with a protein bar or drink, we also risk missing out on the rich sources of antioxidants(抗氧化剂), vitamins and many other benefits of real food.", "52": "Do you ever blend up a protein drink for breakfast, or grab a protein bar following an afternoon workout? If so, you are likely among the millions of people in search of more protein-rich diets.\nProtein-enriched products are found everywhere. But contrary to all the publicity that everyone needs more protein, most Americans get twice as much as they need.\nMany of us living in the most developed countries are buying into a myth of protein deficiency created by food companies and self-identified health experts. Global retail sales of protein supplement products reached an astonishing US$18.9 billion in 2020.\nBut are we really in need of more protein? Physicians in the U.S. have never actually examined a patient with protein deficiency because simply by eating an adequate number of daily calories we are also most likely getting enough protein.\nIn fact, Americans currently consume almost twice the National Academy of Medicine's recommended daily intake of protein although the most desirable protein intake may vary depending on age and activity level.\nFor example, if you're a dedicated athlete you might need to consume higher quantities of protein. Generally, though, a 140-pound person should not exceed 120 grams of protein per day, particularly because a high protein diet can strain kidney and liver function and increase risks of developing heart disease and cancer.\nWhile fats and sugar have taken the beating in turns since over a century ago, protein has managed to remain our red-hot favorite.\nIn the 1970s through the 1990s, protein products remained visible but moved back somewhat with the dietary spotlight firmly fixed on low-calorie, low-fat, sugar-free snack foods and beverages following the publication of studies linking sugar and saturated(饱和的) fat consumption to heart disease.\nLater research in 2003, however, suggested high-protein diets could aid in weight loss, and protein quickly regained its former nutrient-superstar status.\nNow most people living in high-income nations are consuming enough protein. When we replace meals with a protein bar or drink, we also risk missing out on the rich sources of antioxidants(抗氧化剂), vitamins and many other benefits of real food.", "53": "Do you ever blend up a protein drink for breakfast, or grab a protein bar following an afternoon workout? If so, you are likely among the millions of people in search of more protein-rich diets.\nProtein-enriched products are found everywhere. But contrary to all the publicity that everyone needs more protein, most Americans get twice as much as they need.\nMany of us living in the most developed countries are buying into a myth of protein deficiency created by food companies and self-identified health experts. Global retail sales of protein supplement products reached an astonishing US$18.9 billion in 2020.\nBut are we really in need of more protein? Physicians in the U.S. have never actually examined a patient with protein deficiency because simply by eating an adequate number of daily calories we are also most likely getting enough protein.\nIn fact, Americans currently consume almost twice the National Academy of Medicine's recommended daily intake of protein although the most desirable protein intake may vary depending on age and activity level.\nFor example, if you're a dedicated athlete you might need to consume higher quantities of protein. Generally, though, a 140-pound person should not exceed 120 grams of protein per day, particularly because a high protein diet can strain kidney and liver function and increase risks of developing heart disease and cancer.\nWhile fats and sugar have taken the beating in turns since over a century ago, protein has managed to remain our red-hot favorite.\nIn the 1970s through the 1990s, protein products remained visible but moved back somewhat with the dietary spotlight firmly fixed on low-calorie, low-fat, sugar-free snack foods and beverages following the publication of studies linking sugar and saturated(饱和的) fat consumption to heart disease.\nLater research in 2003, however, suggested high-protein diets could aid in weight loss, and protein quickly regained its former nutrient-superstar status.\nNow most people living in high-income nations are consuming enough protein. When we replace meals with a protein bar or drink, we also risk missing out on the rich sources of antioxidants(抗氧化剂), vitamins and many other benefits of real food.", "54": "Do you ever blend up a protein drink for breakfast, or grab a protein bar following an afternoon workout? If so, you are likely among the millions of people in search of more protein-rich diets.\nProtein-enriched products are found everywhere. But contrary to all the publicity that everyone needs more protein, most Americans get twice as much as they need.\nMany of us living in the most developed countries are buying into a myth of protein deficiency created by food companies and self-identified health experts. Global retail sales of protein supplement products reached an astonishing US$18.9 billion in 2020.\nBut are we really in need of more protein? Physicians in the U.S. have never actually examined a patient with protein deficiency because simply by eating an adequate number of daily calories we are also most likely getting enough protein.\nIn fact, Americans currently consume almost twice the National Academy of Medicine's recommended daily intake of protein although the most desirable protein intake may vary depending on age and activity level.\nFor example, if you're a dedicated athlete you might need to consume higher quantities of protein. Generally, though, a 140-pound person should not exceed 120 grams of protein per day, particularly because a high protein diet can strain kidney and liver function and increase risks of developing heart disease and cancer.\nWhile fats and sugar have taken the beating in turns since over a century ago, protein has managed to remain our red-hot favorite.\nIn the 1970s through the 1990s, protein products remained visible but moved back somewhat with the dietary spotlight firmly fixed on low-calorie, low-fat, sugar-free snack foods and beverages following the publication of studies linking sugar and saturated(饱和的) fat consumption to heart disease.\nLater research in 2003, however, suggested high-protein diets could aid in weight loss, and protein quickly regained its former nutrient-superstar status.\nNow most people living in high-income nations are consuming enough protein. When we replace meals with a protein bar or drink, we also risk missing out on the rich sources of antioxidants(抗氧化剂), vitamins and many other benefits of real food.", "55": "Do you ever blend up a protein drink for breakfast, or grab a protein bar following an afternoon workout? If so, you are likely among the millions of people in search of more protein-rich diets.\nProtein-enriched products are found everywhere. But contrary to all the publicity that everyone needs more protein, most Americans get twice as much as they need.\nMany of us living in the most developed countries are buying into a myth of protein deficiency created by food companies and self-identified health experts. Global retail sales of protein supplement products reached an astonishing US$18.9 billion in 2020.\nBut are we really in need of more protein? Physicians in the U.S. have never actually examined a patient with protein deficiency because simply by eating an adequate number of daily calories we are also most likely getting enough protein.\nIn fact, Americans currently consume almost twice the National Academy of Medicine's recommended daily intake of protein although the most desirable protein intake may vary depending on age and activity level.\nFor example, if you're a dedicated athlete you might need to consume higher quantities of protein. Generally, though, a 140-pound person should not exceed 120 grams of protein per day, particularly because a high protein diet can strain kidney and liver function and increase risks of developing heart disease and cancer.\nWhile fats and sugar have taken the beating in turns since over a century ago, protein has managed to remain our red-hot favorite.\nIn the 1970s through the 1990s, protein products remained visible but moved back somewhat with the dietary spotlight firmly fixed on low-calorie, low-fat, sugar-free snack foods and beverages following the publication of studies linking sugar and saturated(饱和的) fat consumption to heart disease.\nLater research in 2003, however, suggested high-protein diets could aid in weight loss, and protein quickly regained its former nutrient-superstar status.\nNow most people living in high-income nations are consuming enough protein. When we replace meals with a protein bar or drink, we also risk missing out on the rich sources of antioxidants(抗氧化剂), vitamins and many other benefits of real food."}, "questions": [{"id": "2024-12-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurate", "B) allow", "C) artificial", "D) cheating", "E) deserted", "F) establish", "G) extremely", "H) immediately", "I) incorrectly", "J) normal", "K) observed", "L) passed", "M) reminding", "N) reparable", "O) resolve"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) normal\n形容词\n【词性槽】showed a + ___ + face，名词 face 前缺修饰词\n【依据】与 'a person wearing a mask' 形成「真脸 vs 戴面具」对照\ndecide which showed a __(26)__ face and which showed a person wearing a mask 后\n半句已经指明对照面是「戴面具的人」，前半句必然是「没戴面具、正常人脸」的意思，\nnormal（正常的）正好表达「未经面具伪装的真实人脸」。\n【竞争词】A) accurate accurate 修饰判断/数据，不修饰 face\naccurate 意为「准确的」，常修饰 description、answer、data，搭配 face 极其别扭，且无法\n与「戴面具」形成对照。\nC) artificial 方向反了，artificial 反而是面具一侧\nartificial（人造的、假的）应当对应「面具脸」，但这里要的恰恰是与「戴面具」相对的「真\n脸」，方向反了；artificial 会留给后面 __(28)__ 用。\nE) deserted deserted 修饰场所，与 face 不搭\ndeserted 多指「废弃的街道/房子」，不和 face 搭配，主体考生只要记住它常修饰地点就能\n排掉。\nN) reparable 「可修复的」修饰损坏物，不修饰人脸\nreparable 意为「可修复的」，对象是机器、损伤、错误，跟「真人脸」语义毫无连接。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurate", "B) allow", "C) artificial", "D) cheating", "E) deserted", "F) establish", "G) extremely", "H) immediately", "I) incorrectly", "J) normal", "K) observed", "L) passed", "M) reminding", "N) reparable", "O) resolve"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) observed\n动词\n【词性槽】名词短语 + ___ + in the study，过去分词作后置定语\n【依据】observe sth. in the study 是研究类文本的常见说法，被动语义贴合\n「研究中观察到的 20% 误差率」直接对应 'The 20% error rate observed in the study'，\nobserved 是 observe（观察/注意到）的过去分词，与 in the study 构成自然的「在研究\n中被观察到的」语义；下文 participants made the wrong call in one-in-five cases 正是\n这一数据的实验来源。\n【竞争词】L) passed passed 与 error rate 搭配不合理\npassed 常搭 pass an exam/test 或 pass sth. off as（冒充），与「误差率」无法构成「pass\n+ in the study」的合理被动结构；它会留到 __(31)__ 那里发挥 passed sb. off as 的固定搭配\n作用。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurate", "B) allow", "C) artificial", "D) cheating", "E) deserted", "F) establish", "G) extremely", "H) immediately", "I) incorrectly", "J) normal", "K) observed", "L) passed", "M) reminding", "N) reparable", "O) resolve"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) artificial\n形容词\n【词性槽】an + ___ + face，再一次「冠词 + 空 + 名词」的形容词槽\n【依据】与 the real thing 形成「假 vs 真」精准对照\ntell an __(28)__ face from the real thing 字面就是「把 ___ 脸与真东西区分开」，与 the\nreal thing 直接相对的就是 artificial（人造的、假的）；上下文 Super realistic masks ...\ndesigned to imitate real human face 一直在讨论「人造仿真脸」这一主题，呼应完美。\n【竞争词】A) accurate accurate 不与 real 形成对照\naccurate 是「准确的」，与 the real thing 没有反义关系，构成不了 tell A from B 的对照\n逻辑。\nE) deserted deserted 修饰场所，与 face 搭配不当\ndeserted（废弃的）几乎只修饰地点/建筑，配 face 既不搭配也不形成对照。\nN) reparable 「可修复的脸」语义荒诞\nreparable（可修复的）对象是损坏物，不修饰人脸；且与 real 不构成对照。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 A) —— 看到 'an ___ face' 误认 accurate「精确的脸」。考生若忽\n视 tell an __(28)__ face from the real thing 的 tell A from B 对照，就可能凭 a + 形容词\n+ face 直接抓 accurate；防坑：先找 from 后面的已知端（the real thing），再到候选里\n挑它的反义形容词 artificial。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurate", "B) allow", "C) artificial", "D) cheating", "E) deserted", "F) establish", "G) extremely", "H) immediately", "I) incorrectly", "J) normal", "K) observed", "L) passed", "M) reminding", "N) reparable", "O) resolve"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) establish\n动词\n【词性槽】to + ___ + any differences，不定式 to 后缺动词原形\n【依据】establish + differences：查明/确认是否存在差异\nThe researchers collected data from participants from both the UK and Japan to\n__(29)__ any differences according to race 表「研究者收集两国数据，以便查明是否存在\n因种族不同而产生的差异」，establish 有「确立、查明、证实」之义，与「研究目的」直\n接对应；后文 response times were slower and selections were 5% less __(30)__ 即「查\n明后得出的差异」。\n【竞争词】O) resolve resolve 是「解决问题」，不是「查明差异」\nresolve 意为「解决/化解」，搭配是 resolve a problem/conflict；「解决差异」与研究语境不\n符——研究者是要「弄清是否存在差异」，而非「化解差异」。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurate", "B) allow", "C) artificial", "D) cheating", "E) deserted", "F) establish", "G) extremely", "H) immediately", "I) incorrectly", "J) normal", "K) observed", "L) passed", "M) reminding", "N) reparable", "O) resolve"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) accurate\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 were + 5% less + ___，less 后修饰程度的形容词\n【依据】selections 5% less accurate：选择结果准确率低 5%\nresponse times were slower and selections were 5% less __(30)__ 与前半句「反应时间\n更慢」是并列两项负面表现；accurate（准确的）正好对接「判断/选择」类主语，整体语\n义是「跨种族判断时选择准确率低 5%」，呼应 participants made the wrong call in\none-in-five cases 的误判主线。\n【竞争词】E) deserted deserted 修饰场所，与 selections 风马牛不相及\ndeserted（废弃的）只搭配地点/建筑，「废弃的选择」语义不通。\nN) reparable 「可修复的选择」不成立，且与对照逻辑无关\nreparable（可修复的）对象是损坏物，搭 selections 完全说不通；本题要的是「准确度高低」\n的对照。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurate", "B) allow", "C) artificial", "D) cheating", "E) deserted", "F) establish", "G) extremely", "H) immediately", "I) incorrectly", "J) normal", "K) observed", "L) passed", "M) reminding", "N) reparable", "O) resolve"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) passed\n动词\n【词性槽】have + ___ + themselves off as，完成时被动后接 off as 的固定搭配\n【依据】passed themselves off as：把自己冒充成另一种身份\noffenders have passed themselves off as people of a different age, race or gender 译\n为「罪犯把自己冒充成不同年龄、种族或性别的人」，与下文 sending police\ninvestigations down the wrong path 「误导警方调查」的逻辑链条扣得严丝合缝。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurate", "B) allow", "C) artificial", "D) cheating", "E) deserted", "F) establish", "G) extremely", "H) immediately", "I) incorrectly", "J) normal", "K) observed", "L) passed", "M) reminding", "N) reparable", "O) resolve"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) extremely\n副词\n【词性槽】an + ___ + 形容词 realistic，缺一个修饰形容词的副词\n【依据】唯一能合理修饰 realistic 程度的程度副词\n整篇文章一直在强调面具「逼真到极致」的特点，imitate real human face—down to\nevery last detail，an __(32)__ realistic mask 自然取「极其逼真」之意，extremely 是程\n度副词，与 realistic 直接构成 'extremely realistic'（极其逼真）这一高频搭配。\n【竞争词】H) immediately 时间副词，与 realistic 语义不搭\nimmediately 表「立刻、立即」，是时间副词，用来修饰动作的发生时点，an __(32)__ realistic\nmask 这里不是说面具「立刻」逼真，逻辑不通。\nI) incorrectly 方式副词且方向相反，不可能修饰 realistic\nincorrectly 是「错误地、不正确地」，本身和 realistic（逼真的）方向相反，「错误地逼真」语\n义自相矛盾；它后面要修饰的应是动作类成分，这里也派不上用场。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurate", "B) allow", "C) artificial", "D) cheating", "E) deserted", "F) establish", "G) extremely", "H) immediately", "I) incorrectly", "J) normal", "K) observed", "L) passed", "M) reminding", "N) reparable", "O) resolve"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) cheating\n动词\n【词性槽】逗号后紧贴主句的伴随性动名词短语，缺一个 -ing 形式动词\n【依据】正好套进 cheat sb. out of money 的固定搭配，且与「冒充行骗」逻辑吻合\n__(33)__ business executives out of millions of pounds 完全是 'cheat sb. out of\nsth.'（骗某人钱财）的固定结构，意思是「骗走商界高管数百万英镑」；offenders have\n__(31)__ themselves off as people of a different age, race or gender 上下文也明确指向\n「行骗」这条主线。\n【竞争词】M) reminding 搭配是 remind sb. of sth.，不接 out of money\nreminding 意为「提醒、使想起」，常见搭配是 'remind sb. of/about sth.'，不可能搭 'out of\nmillions of pounds'；与「行骗」语义也不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurate", "B) allow", "C) artificial", "D) cheating", "E) deserted", "F) establish", "G) extremely", "H) immediately", "I) incorrectly", "J) normal", "K) observed", "L) passed", "M) reminding", "N) reparable", "O) resolve"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) allow\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 may 后缺动词原形\n【依据】allow sth. to be done：使关键特征得以被（错误地）识别\nmasks may __(34)__ the key characteristics of a person's appearance to be __(35)__\nidentified 完整成「面具可能让一个人外表的关键特征被错误地识别」，对应「allow + 宾\n+ to be done」固定搭配；且 Failure to detect synthetic faces may have important\nimplications for security and crime prevention 的「负面后果」语义也由 allow 承担\n——是面具「使得」误识别成为可能。\n【竞争词】F) establish 不接 sth. to be done 结构，语义也偏正面\nestablish 意为「确立、查明」，搭配 establish sth.（事实/制度），不能跟 'sth. to be done'\n这种使役结构；且 establish 强调「确认正确的事实」，与下文「被错误识别」语义冲突。\nO) resolve resolve 是「解决问题」，与「让特征被识别」无关\nresolve 意为「解决（问题/争端）」，常搭 resolve a problem/dispute，与本句「面具让特征\n被识别」逻辑无连接，且也不接 'sth. to be done'。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurate", "B) allow", "C) artificial", "D) cheating", "E) deserted", "F) establish", "G) extremely", "H) immediately", "I) incorrectly", "J) normal", "K) observed", "L) passed", "M) reminding", "N) reparable", "O) resolve"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) incorrectly\n副词\n【词性槽】to be + ___ + identified，被动语态里修饰过去分词需要副词\n【依据】与「面具威胁识别、辨认失败」的负面主旨完全对齐\nFailure to detect synthetic faces may have important implications for security and\ncrime prevention 已经定调是「识别失败」；面具会让关键面部特征被 incorrectly（错误\n地）识别，正是「认错人」的核心威胁；与 participants made the wrong call in one-in-\nfive cases 「误判率高」的实验数据也相互呼应。\n【竞争词】H) immediately immediately 表「立刻」，与文章「识别错误」主旨不符\nimmediately（立刻、立即）只表时间速度，「特征被立刻识别」反而是好事，与 Failure to\ndetect synthetic faces 的负面忧虑方向反了。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— 看到 to be ___ identified 误选 immediately「立刻被识\n别」。to be __(35)__ identified 中 immediately 词性也对、语义看着合理，但与 Failure\nto detect synthetic faces may have important implications for security and crime\nprevention 的负面主旨方向相反；防坑：在词性已定的两个副词之间，看段落是「担忧/批\n评」还是「赞扬」，担忧场景取负面 incorrectly。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Acquisition of new knowledge may not guarantee the progress that you aim to make.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】new knowledge ↔ progress 的否定连接\n【定位】What I'm starting to realize, however, is that new knowledge does not necessarily drive\nnew results.\n然而，我开始意识到的是：新的知识未必能带来新的结果。\n【改写】Acquisition of new knowledge ↔ new knowledge\n名词化等价\nmay not guarantee ↔ does not necessarily drive\n弱否定同义替换\nthe progress that you aim to make ↔ new results\n上下位等价\n【辨邻】 B B 段是 We assume that if we knew about a better strategy, then we would get\nbetter results，只是把 大众的设想 摆出来；陈述句要的是 不能保证 的判断，这一判\n断只出现在 C 段。\nWe believe that a new result requires new knowledge\nK K 段的 the main point of this article is that learning by itself does not lead to\nprogress 是 收束重申，本文 反直觉新结论 的首次提出在 C 段。\nlearning by itself does not lead to progress", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Your current situation in life is built on the beliefs and habits you have been practicing daily.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】current situation = beliefs and habits\n【定位】The state of your life right now is a result of the habits and beliefs that you have been\npracticing each day.\n你此刻的人生状态，是你每天所践行的习惯和信念的结果。\n【改写】Your current situation in life ↔ The state of your life right now\n同义短语替换\nbeliefs and habits ↔ habits and beliefs\n并列项顺序调换\npracticing daily ↔ practicing each day\n副词与短语等值", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Knowing the best way to learn a foreign language will not enable you to speak it without actual practice.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】foreign language 案例\n【定位】For example, let's say you want to learn a foreign language. Reading a book on how to learn a\nforeign language quickly allows you to feel like you are making progress (\"Hey, I'm figuring\nout the best way to do this!\"). Of course, you're not actually practicing the action that would\ndeliver your desired outcome (speaking the foreign language).\n比如，假设你想学一门外语。读一本 怎样快速学外语 的书会让你觉得自己在进步（嘿，我\n正在搞清楚最好的方法！）。但其实，你并没有在实际练习那能带来你想要的结果的那个动\n作——说这门外语。\n【改写】Knowing the best way to learn a foreign language ↔\nReading a book on how to learn a foreign language quickly\n学习手段同位替换\nwill not enable you to speak it without actual practice ↔\nyou're not actually practicing the action that would deliver your desired outcome (speaking\nthe foreign language)\n否定关系等价\n【辨邻】 E E 段的例子是 get stronger and more fit、grow your startup、write a book，与\nforeign language 无关。\nLet's say your goal is to get stronger and more fit", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "People may find it demanding to create a goal which will take a long time to achieve.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】长时间才能达成的目标 + demanding\n【定位】Even so, trying to set achievable goals that you'll work for over the course of many years can\nbe extremely challenging.\n即便如此，要为你需要奋斗多年才能达成的目标设立可实现的方案，可能极其困难。\n【改写】create a goal ↔ to set achievable goals\n动宾同义替换\nwhich will take a long time to achieve ↔ over the course of many years\n时长表达同义替换\ndemanding ↔ extremely challenging\n形容词等价", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Learning and practicing seem to be very much alike, but the two processes can lead to quite different outcomes.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】alike but different outcomes\n【定位】Learning something new and practicing something new may seem very similar, but these two\nmethods can have profoundly different results.\n学新东西与练新东西看起来非常相似，但这两种方式可能带来极为不同的结果。\n【改写】Learning and practicing seem to be very much alike ↔\nLearning something new and practicing something new may seem very similar\n同义重述\nthe two processes can lead to quite different outcomes ↔\nthese two methods can have profoundly different results\n整句同义重述\n【辨邻】 H H 段是 Another point to consider is that practice is learning, but learning is not\npractice——讨论的是包含关系，不是 看起来相似但结果不同。\npractice is learning, but learning is not practice", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Absorbing new information is helpful in making better decisions prior to action.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】absorbing information → better decisions\n【定位】Not to mention that soaking up new information can help you make more informed decisions\nwhen you do decide to take action.\n更不用说，吸收新的信息有助于你在真正决定行动时做出更明智的决策。\n【改写】Absorbing new information ↔ soaking up new information\n动词族同义替换\nhelpful in making better decisions ↔ help you make more informed decisions\n形容词转动词同义\nprior to action ↔ when you do decide to take action\n时间状语同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Sometimes we may feel like we are making progress by doing some research or making preparations, but actually we are staying at the starting point.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】feel like progress but actually staying at starting point\n【定位】In situations like this one, we often claim that we are preparing or researching the best\nmethod, but these rationalizations allow us to feel like we are moving forward when we are\nmerely spinning our wheels.\n在这种情形下，我们常声称自己在做准备或研究最佳方法，但这些自我合理化让我们觉得自\n己在前进，其实只是原地空转。\n【改写】doing some research or making preparations ↔ preparing or researching the best method\n并列动词同义替换\nfeel like we are making progress ↔ feel like we are moving forward\n短语等价\nstaying at the starting point ↔ spinning our wheels\n比喻与字面同义\n【辨邻】 F F 段的 Reading a book on how to learn a foreign language quickly allows you\nto feel like you are making progress 是 具体例子，陈述句 Q42 的 评论性概括 出现\n在承接段 G。\nReading a book on how to learn a foreign language quickly allows you to feel like you are\nmaking progress", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "We are inclined to think that a lack of knowledge prevents us from advancing to a better future.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】lack of knowledge 阻碍进步\n【定位】It can be easy to assume that the gap between where you are now and where you want to be in\nthe future is caused by a lack of knowledge.\n我们很容易这样设想：你当下所处的位置与你将来想到达的位置之间的差距，是由知识不足\n造成的。\n【改写】We are inclined to think ↔ It can be easy to assume\n倾向表达同义\na lack of knowledge prevents us from advancing to a better future ↔\nthe gap between where you are now and where you want to be in the future is caused by a lack\nof knowledge\n因果链对位\n【辨邻】 C C 段以 What I'm starting to realize, however 开头是 反驳的转折，与陈述句 倾向于\n认为 的口吻相反。\nWhat I'm starting to realize, however, is that new knowledge does not necessarily drive new results", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Learning can benefit you by itself, but only practice can turn what you learn into meaningful contributions.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】for you vs meaningful contributions\n【定位】Learning by itself can be valuable for you, but if you want to be valuable to others, then you\nhave to express your knowledge in some way.\n学习本身对你自己可能有价值，但如果你想对他人产生价值，就必须以某种方式把你所学的\n表达出来。\n【改写】Learning can benefit you by itself ↔ Learning by itself can be valuable for you\n同义重述\nonly practice can turn what you learn into meaningful contributions ↔\npractice is the only way to make a meaningful contribution with your knowledge\n唯一路径同义重述\n【辨邻】 H H 段的 Passive learning is not a form of practice 是 包含关系判断，不涉及\nmeaningful contribution 这一价值出口。\nPassive learning is not a form of practice because although you gain new knowledge, you are not\ndiscovering how to apply that knowledge", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Practice involves repeating a process intentionally to attain a particular objective.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】练习的定义：重复以达目标\n【定位】He says: \"When we practice something, we are involved in the deliberate repetition of a\nprocess with the intention of reaching a specific goal.\"\n他说：当我们练习某事时，我们是在带着达成某个具体目标的意图，刻意地重复一个过程。\n【改写】repeating a process ↔ repetition of a process\n动名词与名词同义\nintentionally ↔ deliberate repetition of a process with the intention of reaching a specific goal\n副词与原文 deliberate/intention 对应\nto attain a particular objective ↔ reaching a specific goal\n动宾同义替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn from research in human-vehicle interaction?", "options": ["A) Automatic driving systems are by no means immune to errors.", "B) Driverless vehicles are likely to be misused by some people.", "C) Self-driving car manufacturers are not aware of the legal matters involved.", "D) There is a long way to go before humans can interact with driverless vehicles."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from research in human-vehicle interaction? 直接问研究告诉了我们\n什么，答案必然是原文一句陈述的客观转述，不需要跨段推理。\n【拆句】结构平铺直叙：主句是 What do we learn from research in human-vehicle\ninteraction，from 引导的介宾短语限定信息来源，无从句、无否定、无陷阱。\n【定位】Research in human-vehicle interaction has shown even systems designed to automate driving\nare far from being error-proof.\n人车交互领域的研究表明，即便是为自动驾驶而设计的系统，也远远谈不上无差错。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nsystems designed to automate driving ↔ Automatic driving systems\nfar from being error-proof ↔ by no means immune to errors\n预判要的是系统并非无差错，A 项 Automatic driving systems are by no means\nimmune to errors 是对 systems designed to automate driving are far from being\nerror-proof 的逐部分同义替换，无添加无减少，锁定 A。\nB) 偷换概念\nDriverless vehicles are likely to be misused by some people 谈的是车被滥用，而题\n干问的是研究发现，定位句结论是系统易出错，两者不在同一语义层；滥用确实在\nsystem misuse 中提到但只是后续展开，并非首句的研究结论。\nC) 无中生有\nSelf-driving car manufacturers are not aware of the legal matters involved 称厂商\n对法律毫无意识，而原文 A recent study tackles the issue…from a legal viewpoint\n只是说有研究从法律角度切入，从未说厂商不知情。\nD) 偷换概念\nThere is a long way to go before humans can interact with driverless vehicles 把\n研究结论说成人车交互本身遥不可及，而原文人车交互只是研究领域名，研究真正的发\n现是系统易错，与是否能交互无关。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What is the problem with requiring buyers to sign end-user license agreements?", "options": ["A) End users, young and old alike, find the terms complex to interpret.", "B) Most end users sign them without bothering to read the terms.", "C) Many people are often confused by the wording of the terms.", "D) Most end users do not understand the terms after reading them through."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is the problem with requiring buyers to sign end-user license agreements? 询\n问一个具体做法的问题所在，属于对原文局部反面描述的提取。\n【拆句】结构清晰：What is the problem with… 主句问的是 problem，with 后跟动名词短语\nrequiring buyers to sign end-user license agreements 作 problem 的定语，整体无\n难点。\n【定位】Further, most end users don't read EULAs. A 2017 study shows 91 percent of people agree to\nthem without reading.\n更何况，大多数终端用户根本不读 EULA。2017 年的一项研究表明，91% 的人未读就同\n意了。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nEnd users, young and old alike, find the terms complex to interpret 说所有人都觉\n得条款难以解读，但原文只说大家压根不读，从未说他们读了之后觉得难懂。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmost end users don't read EULAs ↔\nMost end users sign them without bothering to read the terms\n91 percent of people agree to them without reading ↔ Most end users\n预判要的是签前不读，B 项 Most end users sign them without bothering to read\nthe terms 正是这一行为的标准表述，与原文双句合并对应，锁定 B。\nC) 偷换概念\nMany people are often confused by the wording of the terms 说人们被措辞搞晕，\n但原文 leading to confusion about the nature of the requests for agreement and\ntheir implications 说的是对协议性质与含义的困惑，且这是协议信息不足的后果，不是\n本题题干问的 EULA 整体的根本问题。\nD) 与原文相反\nMost end users do not understand the terms after reading them through 隐含的\n前提是用户把条款读完了，只是不懂；而原文 most end users don't read EULAs 的事\n实是用户根本没读，两者前提相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What would drivers do when they suffer from \"warning fatigue\"?", "options": ["A) Waste no time keeping the car moving.", "B) Rest a while to avoid fatigue driving.", "C) Take no action despite repeated warnings.", "D) Take note of the message though fatigued."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What would drivers do when they suffer from \"warning fatigue\"? 问的是在某情境下司\n机会做出何种动作，属于对原文具体情境的事实提取。\n【拆句】主句 What would drivers do 是常规疑问句，when 引导的时间状语从句 when they\nsuffer from \"warning fatigue\" 限定情境，suffer from 意为遭受、深受其扰，无陷阱。\n【定位】For example, a driver, annoyed after receiving continuous warnings, could decide to just\nignore the message.\n举例来说，一名司机在接到接连不断的警告后心生厌烦，可能干脆决定无视这条信息。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nWaste no time keeping the car moving 是 抓紧时间让车继续走，与原文 ignore the\nmessage 描述的不作为完全不是一回事。\nB) 偷换概念\nRest a while to avoid fatigue driving 在谈疲劳驾驶要休息，但原文 \"Warning\nfatigue\" 指对警告厌烦，并非生理疲劳。\nC) ✓ 正确\ncould decide to just ignore the message ↔ Take no action despite repeated warnings\n预判要的是无视警告这一行为，C 项 Take no action despite repeated warnings 把无\n视＝不作为、反复警告＝重复警告两端都精准对位，锁定 C。\nD) 与原文相反\nTake note of the message though fatigued 是 虽然疲惫但仍留意警告，与原文 just\nignore the message 完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author think of continuing to ask buyers to sign end-user license agreements?", "options": ["A) It will probably not guarantee the safety of drivers in case of accidents.", "B) It likely won't ensure that the automatic driving system functions properly.", "C) It likely won't fully protect automakers against accusations of deliberate cheating.", "D) It will probably not provide manufacturers adequate protection from legal responsibilities."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think of continuing to ask buyers to sign end-user license\nagreements? 虽问作者看法，但锁定于 EULAs 这一局部做法，仍以一句明确表态句为答案\n依据，属细节题。\n【拆句】What does the author think of continuing to ask buyers to sign end-user license\nagreements? 中 think of 后接动名词 continuing，continuing 再接动词不定式 to ask\nbuyers to sign…，是动名词+不定式连用的链式结构，意为对继续要求买家签 EULA 这件\n事，作者怎么看。\n【定位】Given these limitations and concerns, even if this mode of obtaining consent is to move\nforward, it likely won't fully protect automakers from their legal liability should the system\nmalfunction(发生故障) or an accident occur.\n鉴于这些局限与担忧，即使继续推行这种取得同意的方式，万一系统发生故障或事故发生，\n它也很可能无法在法律责任上充分保护汽车制造商。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nIt will probably not guarantee the safety of drivers in case of accidents 谈的是事\n故中司机的安全保障，但原文 protect automakers from their legal liability 谈的是保\n护厂商免于法律责任，对象与内容都错位。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt likely won't ensure that the automatic driving system functions properly 谈的是\n系统能否正常运行，但原文谈的是法律层面对厂商的保护，二者根本不是一回事。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt likely won't fully protect automakers against accusations of deliberate cheating\n谈的是免于被指故意欺诈，但原文 legal liability 仅指法律责任，未涉及 deliberate\ncheating 故意欺诈这一更窄、更带主观恶意的范畴。\nD) ✓ 正确\nit likely won't fully protect automakers from their legal liability ↔\nIt will probably not provide manufacturers adequate protection from legal responsibilities\nautomakers ↔ manufacturers\n预判要的是不能充分保护厂商的法律责任，D 项 It will probably not provide\nmanufacturers adequate protection from legal responsibilities 与定位句逐字对\n位，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What should be done to help drivers fully understand system capabilities and limitations?", "options": ["A) Training them to be experts in vehicle automation.", "B) Familiarizing them with the systems through training.", "C) Broadening their knowledge of accident-prevention mechanisms.", "D) Facilitating their access to the information provided by the seller."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What should be done to help drivers fully understand system capabilities and\nlimitations? 直接问该做什么以达成目的，属于对原文解决方案句的提取。\n【拆句】主句 What should be done 是被动疑问句，to help 引导的不定式作目的状语，help\ndrivers 后接动词原形 fully understand…，整句意思是：为帮司机充分理解系统能力与局\n限，应该做什么。\n【定位】Driver training for self-driving vehicles can help ensure that drivers fully understand system\ncapabilities and limitations.\n针对自动驾驶汽车的司机培训，能够帮助确保司机充分了解系统的能力与局限。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nTraining them to be experts in vehicle automation 把目标拔到成为车辆自动化领域\n专家的高度，而原文只要求 fully understand system capabilities and limitations，是\n理解层级而非专家层级。\nB) ✓ 正确\nDriver training for self-driving vehicles can help ensure that drivers fully understand system\ncapabilities and limitations\n↔ Familiarizing them with the systems through training\nfully understand system capabilities and limitations ↔ Familiarizing them with the systems\n预判要的是通过培训让司机熟悉系统，B 项 Familiarizing them with the systems\nthrough training 与定位句双层等值，锁定 B。\nC) 无中生有\nBroadening their knowledge of accident-prevention mechanisms 谈的是扩大防事\n故机制的知识面，但原文谈的是理解系统的能力与局限，accident-prevention\nmechanisms 在文中并未出现。\nD) 与原文相反\nFacilitating their access to the information provided by the seller 主张方便司机获\n取卖家信息，但原文 even relying on the information provided by the seller is not\ngoing to answer many questions 明确指出哪怕依赖卖家信息也无法解答许多问题，\n二者方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn about publicity over protein in America?", "options": ["A) It has helped to create the myth of protein deficiency.", "B) It has helped Americans in search of protein-rich diets.", "C) It has raised a lot of health concerns among the public.", "D) It has been funded by food companies and health experts."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about publicity over protein in America? 是一个 What…learn\nabout 的事实型问句，直接问关于某对象（publicity）的某一事实，没有\ninfer/suggest/imply 等推断信号。\n【拆句】句式平铺直叙：What do we learn about publicity over protein in America? 主干是\nWhat do we learn about X，介词短语 over protein 是 publicity 的范围限定，in\nAmerica 限定地域，无陷阱。\n【定位】Many of us living in the most developed countries are buying into a myth of protein\ndeficiency created by food companies and self-identified health experts.\n我们这些生活在大多数发达国家的人，正在接受一个由食品公司和自封的健康专家制造出来\n的\"蛋白质缺乏\"神话。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nbuying into a myth of protein deficiency created by food companies and self-identified health\nexperts\n↔ It has helped to create the myth of protein deficiency.\ncontrary to all the publicity that everyone needs more protein ↔\nthe myth of protein deficiency\n预判说\"宣传促成了蛋白缺乏的迷思\"，helped to create the myth of protein\ndeficiency 一字不差地落到这个语义上，是定位句的直接同义替换。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文用 buying into a myth 表明宣传是在\"骗\"美国人，并非\"帮\"他们；选项 helped\nAmericans in search of protein-rich diets 把负面后果包装成正面服务，与原作者态度\n相反。\nC) 无中生有\n原文从未说 publicity \"raised health concerns\"——P6 的健康风险讲的是高蛋白饮食\n本身，而非舆论造成的恐慌；选项 raised a lot of health concerns among the public\n在文中无落点。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文说迷思是 created by food companies and self-identified health experts，主语\n是\"食品公司和自封专家\"在制造迷思；选项 funded by food companies and health\nexperts 把 created 偷换成 funded（资助），且把 publicity 当受资助对象，主体与动\n作都被改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Why have physicians in the U.S. never actually examined a patient with protein deficiency?", "options": ["A) Americans can purchase many kinds of protein-rich foods everywhere.", "B) Americans can absorb the number of calories recommended by food experts.", "C) Americans can get sufficient protein just by eating adequate foods daily.", "D) Americans can rely on food supplements to prevent protein deficiency."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Why have physicians in the U.S. never actually examined a patient with protein\ndeficiency? 用 Why 直问原因，要求按原文给出的解释作答，不需要自行推断。\n【拆句】现在完成时 have…never actually examined，never 是关键否定词，actually 加强语气，\n提示原文给出的解释是\"压根没机会见到\"层面的原因；理解时把 never examined 当作\"从\n未见过\"即可。\n【定位】Physicians in the U.S. have never actually examined a patient with protein deficiency because\nsimply by eating an adequate number of daily calories we are also most likely getting\nenough protein.\n美国医生从未真正诊治过蛋白缺乏的病人，因为只要每天摄入足够的卡路里，我们也就极可\n能获得了足够的蛋白质。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n原文给出的原因是\"日常热量摄入足够蛋白也就够了\"，与\"能否买到\"无关；选项\npurchase many kinds of protein-rich foods everywhere 谈购买便利性，是另一\n回事。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文说的是 an adequate number of daily calories\"日常足量的卡路里\"，选项 absorb\nthe number of calories recommended by food experts 偷换为\"食品专家推荐的卡路\n里数\"——多了\"food experts recommended\"这一未出现的限定，且 absorb 与\neating 含义偏移。\nC) ✓ 正确\nsimply by eating an adequate number of daily calories we are also most likely getting enough\nprotein\n↔ Americans can get sufficient protein just by eating adequate foods daily.\n预判说\"靠正常饮食的热量就够蛋白\"，get sufficient protein just by eating adequate\nfoods daily 几乎逐字对应，是 because 句的同义替换。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文的因果是\"正常饮食就够蛋白，所以不必靠补剂\"；选项 rely on food supplements\nto prevent protein deficiency 反过来说\"靠补剂预防缺乏\"，正是全文批判的对象。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What should people take into consideration in deciding on the most preferable protein intake?", "options": ["A) How healthy they are and what food they eat.", "B) How old they are and what activities they do.", "C) How much protein they consume and what their body weight is.", "D) How serious their protein deficiency is and what remedies they use."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What should people take into consideration in deciding on the most preferable\nprotein intake? 在问决策依据，原文必会以并列形式给出依据项，照搬即可。\n【拆句】in deciding on… 是 in doing 结构作状语，表\"在做某事的时候\"；主干是 What should\npeople take into consideration，把状语剥掉，问句就回归\"应考虑什么\"，简单。\n【定位】Americans currently consume almost twice the National Academy of Medicine's\nrecommended daily intake of protein although the most desirable protein intake may vary\ndepending on age and activity level.\n尽管最理想的蛋白摄入量可能因年龄和活动量不同而有所差异，美国人现在的蛋白摄入仍接\n近美国医学科学院推荐摄入量的两倍。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只列了 age and activity level，并未把\"现在健康程度\"或\"吃什么食物\"列入考虑因\n素；选项 How healthy they are and what food they eat 是凭常识自造的因素。\nB) ✓ 正确\ndepending on age and activity level ↔ How old they are and what activities they do.\n预判的\"年龄+活动量\"两项，与 How old they are and what activities they do 一一对\n齐，是最直接的同义替换。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文要讨论的是\"理想蛋白摄入量\"本身，选项 How much protein they consume and\nwhat their body weight is 反过来把\"已经摄入多少蛋白\"和\"体重\"作为考量因素，循\n环定义且无文本依据；P6 提到 140-pound 只是举例上限，并非\"决定理想摄入量的\n因素\"。\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 How serious their protein deficiency is and what remedies they use 默认存\n在\"protein deficiency\"，但原文 P4 明确说美国医生从未见过蛋白缺乏患者，根本不存\n在这样的前提。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What foods have been largely out of favor for more than a century?", "options": ["A) Sugar-free snacks.", "B) Protein-rich foods.", "C) Both red and white meats.", "D) Sugary and fatty foods."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What foods have been largely out of favor for more than a century? 是事实型问句，\n答案就在原文某句的事实陈述里。\n【拆句】现在完成时 have been largely out of favor 表过去到现在的持续状态；largely 是程度副\n词，表\"在很大程度上\"，提醒不要选\"完全/彻底\"等绝对化误读。\n【定位】While fats and sugar have taken the beating in turns since over a century ago, protein has\nmanaged to remain our red-hot favorite.\n一个多世纪以来，脂肪和糖轮流挨批，而蛋白质却始终是我们的红人。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文 dietary spotlight firmly fixed on low-calorie, low-fat, sugar-free snack foods\n说\"无糖零食\"是 1970-90 年代受追捧的对象，不是被嫌弃的对象；选项 Sugar-free\nsnacks 把追捧对象当成 out of favor，颠倒立场。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 protein has managed to remain our red-hot favorite 明说蛋白质始终是\nfavorite（红人），选项 Protein-rich foods 反过来说它失宠，180 度反向。\nC) 无中生有\n原文 P7-P9 整段都在讲脂肪、糖和蛋白质这几类宏量营养素，从未提到\"红肉/白肉\"被批\n的话题；选项 Both red and white meats 是凭健康常识填空。\nD) ✓ 正确\nfats and sugar ↔ Sugary and fatty foods.\ntaken the beating in turns since over a century ago ↔\nlargely out of favor for more than a century\n预判\"脂肪和糖被批了一百多年\"，Sugary and fatty foods 对齐对象，题干本身对齐时\n间与态度，三要素全合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-12-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author imply people in developed countries should do to keep healthy?", "options": ["A) Enjoy natural food.", "B) Eat vegetarian diets.", "C) Consume enough protein daily.", "D) Replace meals with protein bars."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】What does the author imply people in developed countries should do to keep\nhealthy? 题干直接用 imply，是推断题的标准句式。\n【拆句】句式较长但分层清晰：主干 What does the author imply X should do，宾语从句的主语\n是 people in developed countries，目的状语 to keep healthy；抓住\"作者+暗示+发达\n国家民众+保持健康\"四要素即可。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nrich sources of antioxidants(抗氧化剂), vitamins and many other benefits of real food ↔\nEnjoy natural food.\nWhen we replace meals with a protein bar or drink, we also risk missing out on… ↔\nEnjoy natural food.\n预判作者推荐\"回到真实食物\"，Enjoy natural food 正是这条推断的最贴文表达，且不\n超过一步推。\nB) 过度推断\n原文只说不要靠补剂代餐、要享受真实食物的好处，从未要求 vegetarian diets（素\n食）；从\"少吃加工补剂\"推不到\"完全吃素\"，跨度过大。\nC) 与原文相反\n全文一直在反驳\"应该多吃蛋白\"的舆论，Americans currently consume almost twice\nthe National Academy of Medicine's recommended daily intake of protein 明示已\n经摄入过量；选项 Consume enough protein daily 仍劝读者每天摄入足够蛋白，与作\n者立场相反。\nD) 与原文相反\n尾段明确告诫 When we replace meals with a protein bar or drink, we also risk\nmissing out on…——\"代餐\"被定性为风险行为；选项 Replace meals with protein\nbars 反过来把它当成建议，立场颠倒。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2024-12-2"});
